window.MH_TR["Ch00_The_Map_of_Coatings"]={"t":"The Map of Coatings","l":[[5.0,"The part came off the milling machine perfect: every dimension in tolerance, and a clean finish."],[11.0,"But if we leave it like this, it won't survive."],[14.0,"in a white powder, and a surface that rubs against another surface wears away."],[23.0,"Machining gives the part its shape. Finishing and coating keep it that way over time."],[28.0,"In this course we'll go through everything that happens to a part after machining:"],[33.0,"how each one works, and how to choose the right one."],[39.0,"There are several reasons to coat a part, and sometimes one coating covers several of them."],[45.0,"The first, and the most common, is protection against corrosion."],[49.0,"The second is wear resistance: a hard coating extends the life of a surface that rubs or wears."],[55.0,"The third is appearance: color, shade, gloss, or a matte look."],[61.0,"The fourth is electrical properties: some coatings conduct very well, like silver and gold on contacts,"],[68.0,"and some coatings insulate, like anodizing."],[71.0,"non-stick, solderability, or restoring the size of a worn part."],[80.0,"look at a cross-section of the surface and ask one question:"],[87.0,"what does the coating do to the base material?"],[89.0,"no material is added at all. Only the surface texture changes."],[97.0,"itself turns into a different compound, through a chemical or electrochemical reaction."],[105.0,"phosphating, black oxide, chromate coating, and anodizing work."],[115.0,"In a metallic coating, a layer of a different metal is deposited on the part:"],[120.0,"with electric current, by a chemical reaction, or by dipping in molten metal."],[125.0,"forms a barrier between the metal and the environment."],[132.0,"PVD, apply materials that the other methods can't, such as ceramics and carbides."],[141.0,"The difference between the families also shows in thickness."],[144.0,"A chromate coating on aluminum is very thin, well under one micron."],[149.0,"A PVD coating on a cutting tool is a few microns thick."],[153.0,"Electroplating and regular anodizing are usually in the range of 5 to 25 microns."],[159.0,"Hard anodizing reaches tens of microns."],[162.0,"more, and thermal spray can reach tenths of a millimeter."],[169.0,"Thickness has a direct meaning on the drawing."],[172.0,"outside diameter by 50 microns, and reduces a bore by the same amount."],[180.0,"On a thread the effect is even bigger:"],[182.0,"because of the thread angle, the pitch diameter changes by about 4 times the coating thickness."],[188.0,"grows inward into the material, and half grows outward."],[194.0,"So you must know whether a dimension on the drawing is before or after coating."],[200.0,"A coating protects against corrosion in two main ways."],[203.0,"The first is a barrier: the coating separates the metal from moisture and oxygen."],[208.0,"That's how paint, nickel, and chrome work."],[211.0,"The drawback is that when a scratch reaches the metal, the protection at that spot is gone."],[216.0,"The second is sacrificial protection."],[219.0,"scratch, the surrounding zinc corrodes instead of the steel and protects it."],[226.0,"That's how zinc plating and galvanizing work."],[229.0,"The same principle also explains galvanic corrosion."],[233.0,"presence of moisture, the more active metal corrodes."],[239.0,"A classic example is a stainless steel screw in an aluminum part, with no insulation."],[245.0,"the question: which metal are we coating, and what touches it?"],[252.0,"How do you choose a coating? Ask five questions."],[255.0,"aluminum, not steel, and passivation suits stainless steel."],[263.0,"indoor space, outdoors, a marine environment, or a chemical environment."],[271.0,"What is the job of the surface? Protection only, wear resistance, electrical contact, or appearance."],[278.0,"thickness the drawing can absorb, and which surfaces must stay uncoated."],[285.0,"And what do the standard or the customer require?"],[288.0,"specified by a standard, with type, class, and thickness, not by free choice."],[299.0,"One issue has been changing the field in recent years, and that's regulation."],[303.0,"Hexavalent chromium is found in chromate coatings and in some primers."],[307.0,"Cadmium was used for many years to protect steel in aerospace."],[312.0,"Both materials are toxic and carcinogenic."],[315.0,"Their use is more and more restricted, for example in Europe under REACH,"],[320.0,"and alternatives are taking their place, such as trivalent chromium coatings and zinc-nickel."],[325.0,"We'll cover this in more detail in chapters 5 and 8."],[329.0,"What makes a coating expensive? Often, it's not the material itself."],[334.0,"Masking means covering the surfaces and threads that must not be coated."],[339.0,"It's manual work, and it can cost more than the coating itself."],[343.0,"Racking matters too: every part needs a holding point or an electrical contact,"],[348.0,"and a part that's hard to hang or to fit into the tank makes the job more expensive."],[353.0,"For a small quantity, you pay a minimum lot charge."],[356.0,"A thick coating, or a system of several layers, takes more time."],[360.0,"order: thickness, adhesion, and salt spray tests, certificates, and approved processes."],[372.0,"The course follows the order a part actually goes through."],[375.0,"finishing, then conversion coatings, metallic coatings, paint, and advanced coatings,"],[384.0,"and finally testing, standards, and designing a part for coating."],[388.0,"which numbers matter, how to design a part for it, what goes wrong, and what drives the cost."],[397.0,"Let's begin."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch01_Cleaning_and_Surface_Preparation"]={"t":"Cleaning and Surface Preparation","l":[[5.0,"Most coating failures don't start with the coating itself, but with what was on the surface before it."],[10.0,"A coating only bonds to clean metal."],[13.0,"underneath, the coating bonds to that layer, not to the part."],[20.0,"Then it will peel, blister, or look perfect until the part goes into service."],[25.0,"That's why every coating process starts with cleaning."],[28.0,"It's the least impressive step, and it's the one that decides whether everything else holds."],[34.0,"several kinds of contamination, and each kind needs a different treatment."],[41.0,"rust preventive oil, polishing compound, and fingerprints."],[50.0,"There's solid contamination: chips, dust, and burrs."],[54.0,"of the metal itself: rust, oxides, and scale from heat treatment or welding."],[63.0,"Soap and water won't remove oxide, and acid won't remove oil."],[67.0,"So cleaning is done in stages: first you remove the oils, and only then the oxides."],[75.0,"The most common method is alkaline cleaning: a hot alkaline solution with surfactants."],[81.0,"Usually between 50 and 80 degrees Celsius, for a few minutes, by immersion or spray."],[87.0,"When there are blind holes, threads, or small parts in bulk, ultrasonics are added."],[93.0,"Sound waves at tens of kilohertz create tiny bubbles that collapse on the surface,"],[98.0,"and pull dirt out of corners the solution alone can't reach."],[102.0,"In electrocleaning, current is passed through the part inside the tank."],[106.0,"aggressively, and it's a standard step before electroplating."],[112.0,"on oils, but solvent use is restricted today for safety and environmental reasons."],[120.0,"After every step the part is rinsed in water, usually more than once."],[124.0,"Any chemical left on the part is dragged into the next tank and contaminates it."],[129.0,"Pickling is dipping the part in an acid that dissolves oxides and rust."],[134.0,"For steel, hydrochloric or sulfuric acid is usually used, with inhibitors,"],[140.0,"so that the acid attacks the oxide more than the metal."],[143.0,"On stainless steel the scale is tougher, and a mix of nitric and hydrofluoric acid is used."],[150.0,"It's a very hazardous solution that requires a suitable facility."],[154.0,"On aluminum, a light alkaline etch is usually done, followed by a desmut:"],[158.0,"removing the dark residue layer left on the surface after etching."],[162.0,"On cast aluminum, with a lot of silicon, this layer is especially noticeable."],[168.0,"And the key rule: acid removes metal."],[171.0,"pitting and loss of size, and with tight tolerances you have to account for it."],[179.0,"hydrogen is released on the steel surface, and some of it penetrates the metal."],[186.0,"In soft steels, this usually isn't a problem."],[189.0,"megapascals, like hardened bolts and springs, hydrogen causes embrittlement."],[198.0,"happens hours or days later under load, when the part is already assembled."],[205.0,"So with these steels, pickling is minimized and mechanical cleaning is preferred."],[210.0,"usually at around 190 to 220 degrees Celsius, for several hours."],[219.0,"And the bake has to start shortly after processing, not days later."],[223.0,"We'll cover this in more detail in chapter 7."],[226.0,"The simplest test is the water break test: take the part out of the rinse and look at the water."],[232.0,"film that stays unbroken for several tens of seconds."],[238.0,"If the water breaks into islands and droplets, there's oil left on the surface."],[243.0,"Steel parts prepared for painting have defined cleanliness grades, according to ISO 8501-1."],[250.0,"Sa 1 is light cleaning, Sa 2 is thorough cleaning,"],[254.0,"common grade for industrial paint, and Sa 3 is white, completely clean metal."],[263.0,"St 2 and St 3 are grades for cleaning with hand and power tools."],[267.0,"American standards have equivalent grades, and we'll cover them in the next chapter, on blasting."],[273.0,"For especially sensitive coatings there are also laboratory tests, and adhesion tests after coating."],[280.0,"solution, and whatever is trapped there doesn't get rinsed out."],[287.0,"coating, causing stains, blisters, and corrosion under the coating."],[295.0,"So, where possible, design drain and vent holes, and avoid closed pockets and unsealed lap joints."],[303.0,"aluminum, clean each part before assembly, because each material needs different chemistry."],[312.0,"cleaning, or a marking that suits the process is chosen in advance."],[319.0,"And one more important thing: time. Clean, dry steel starts to rust within hours."],[326.0,"So clean parts are coated quickly, and handled only with gloves."],[330.0,"Common problems: oil that wasn't removed, causing peeling and blisters."],[335.0,"A residue layer on aluminum, which ruins the conversion coating."],[340.0,"Over-pickling, which causes pitting and loss of size."],[344.0,"Rust that appears between rinsing and coating, and hydrogen embrittlement in hard parts."],[349.0,"polishing compound, heavy rust, or thick scale, need more steps and more time."],[358.0,"Geometry that traps solution needs extra rinses, and sometimes manual cleaning."],[364.0,"A hydrogen relief bake adds hours in the oven for every lot."],[367.0,"And waste treatment: used chemicals cost money, and that cost goes into the price of every part."],[374.0,"The rule to remember: good cleaning is cheaper than any repair of a failed coating."],[380.0,"Next chapter: blasting and mechanical finishing."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch02_Blasting_and_Mechanical_Finishing"]={"t":"Blasting and Mechanical Finishing","l":[[5.0,"After chemical cleaning comes mechanical finishing."],[7.0,"is changed by force: hit with particles, ground, or rubbed."],[15.0,"There are three goals."],[17.0,"To remove things chemistry doesn't easily remove, like scale, heavy rust, and old paint."],[23.0,"To create a texture the coating can grip."],[26.0,"And to give the part a uniform look: matte, satin, or bright."],[30.0,"We'll go through four types: blasting, polishing, brushing, and tumbling."],[35.0,"accelerates hard particles and slams them into the surface."],[42.0,"Every particle that hits leaves a small crater, and carries dirt and oxide away with it."],[47.0,"Small parts are done in a closed cabinet with gloves."],[51.0,"particles by centrifugal force, without compressed air."],[59.0,"and 7 bar, and distance from the part, usually tens of centimeters."],[69.0,"Plus the angle, and the type and size of the particle."],[73.0,"High pressure and large particles remove more, but also leave a rougher surface."],[78.0,"Angular particles, like aluminum oxide, steel grit, and garnet, cut into the surface."],[85.0,"They clean fast and create a texture of peaks and valleys: the best texture for paint adhesion."],[90.0,"Round particles, like steel shot and glass beads, don't cut. They peen the surface."],[96.0,"They remove very little material and give a uniform satin look."],[100.0,"This is the common choice for cosmetic finishing, for example before anodizing."],[105.0,"soda, remove paint without damaging the metal underneath."],[114.0,"They're used in aerospace, to strip paint from thin aluminum and from composites."],[119.0,"causes a severe lung disease, and in many places its use is restricted or banned."],[127.0,"The name sandblasting has stayed in everyday language."],[131.0,"The texture blasting leaves is called the surface profile, or anchor profile."],[136.0,"It's measured as the peak-to-valley height, in microns."],[140.0,"several tens of microns, and the paint specification sets the exact range."],[148.0,"Too shallow a profile, and the paint has nothing to grip."],[151.0,"Too deep a profile, and the peaks stick out through the coating, and that's where corrosion starts."],[156.0,"tape pressed onto the surface, or with a stylus gauge."],[163.0,"In chapter 1 we saw the cleanliness grades, from Sa 1 to Sa 3."],[168.0,"Blasting is how you reach them, and the American equivalents are SSPC-SP7, SP6, SP10, and SP5."],[178.0,"is left on the surface, and the profile says what texture was created."],[187.0,"The two are checked separately, and both need to appear in the specification."],[192.0,"aluminum with media that was used on carbon steel."],[199.0,"after a few days they rust, leaving brown spots on a part that's supposed to be corrosion resistant."],[208.0,"That's why there are separate cabinets and media for each family of materials."],[212.0,"The compressed air also has to be clean and dry."],[215.0,"the part, and takes us back to the problem from chapter 1."],[222.0,"and particles trapped in holes and slots are removed."],[228.0,"And coat the part quickly: blasted steel is clean steel, and it starts to rust within hours."],[235.0,"A side note: there's also a process called shot peening."],[238.0,"compressive stresses in the surface layer, which greatly improves fatigue life."],[247.0,"measurement, and it's different from blasting for cleaning."],[253.0,"In polishing, the grit is reduced in steps, from coarse to fine, finishing with polishing compound."],[259.0,"with an Ra of tenths or even hundredths of a micron."],[268.0,"Compound residue has to be removed before coating, as we saw in chapter 1."],[273.0,"Brushing leaves fine parallel lines in one direction: that's the familiar satin look on stainless steel."],[280.0,"It's important to define the direction of the lines on the drawing, because it affects the appearance."],[285.0,"vibratory bowl, together with ceramic or plastic media, and liquid."],[295.0,"for hours: burrs disappear, corners round off, and the surface smooths out."],[303.0,"It's ideal for small parts in large quantities, because hundreds of parts are processed at once."],[310.0,"Blasting distorts thin parts."],[312.0,"and when a thin sheet is blasted on one side only, it bends."],[319.0,"For such parts, blast both sides, lower the pressure, or choose a finer particle."],[324.0,"Blasting rounds sharp edges and removes a little material. The change is usually small,"],[330.0,"surfaces it matters, and those surfaces are masked."],[336.0,"stuck in holes, and separate delicate parts so they don't damage each other."],[344.0,"or an approved appearance sample, and which surfaces are masked."],[355.0,"The word blasting alone, with no details, leaves every decision to the operator."],[360.0,"stainless steel and aluminum, and distortion of thin parts."],[367.0,"Rounded corners and damaged dimensions from blasting too long."],[371.0,"Uneven appearance between parts, and rust that appears between blasting and coating."],[376.0,"And what drives the cost? Masking: manual work for every part."],[381.0,"operator time per part. In production runs, a machine is much cheaper."],[388.0,"parts needs inspection, and sometimes parts are rejected."],[395.0,"Mirror polishing, with many steps and skilled manual work."],[398.0,"And tumbling is the cheapest per part, provided the parts are small and numerous."],[404.0,"Next chapter: electropolishing and passivation of stainless steel."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch03_Electropolishing_and_Passivation"]={"t":"Electropolishing and Passivation","l":[[5.0,"Stainless steel is steel with at least 10.5% chromium."],[9.0,"forms a very thin oxide layer on the surface, just a few nanometers thick."],[17.0,"This layer is called the passive layer."],[19.0,"It's transparent and tightly bonded, and when it's scratched, it rebuilds itself."],[25.0,"iron on the surface, particles from tools and work surfaces, and oil residue."],[34.0,"In those spots the layer doesn't form properly, and rust spots appear."],[38.0,"There are two processes for this: passivation and electropolishing."],[43.0,"Passivation is dipping the part in acid, usually nitric acid or citric acid."],[49.0,"The acid dissolves the free iron and contaminants from the surface, leaving a chromium-rich surface."],[55.0,"Then a uniform, stable passive layer forms."],[58.0,"appearance, and no measurable change in dimensions."],[64.0,"scale, it doesn't remove weld heat tint, and it doesn't remove oil."],[71.0,"by cleaning, and scale and weld heat tint by pickling."],[79.0,"Nitric acid is the long-established method, and the most common in aerospace."],[84.0,"stainless grade, and the time is usually tens of minutes."],[90.0,"environment, and the main standards approve it as an alternative."],[97.0,"2700. They define the methods by stainless grade, and the tests."],[107.0,"The tests include water immersion or high humidity exposure, checking that no rust appears,"],[114.0,"which reveals free iron, and isn't suitable for every stainless grade."],[122.0,"The austenitic 300 series, like 304 and 316, responds best to passivation."],[128.0,"less chromium and are more sensitive, so the right method must be chosen."],[137.0,"The big problem is free-machining stainless, like 303 and 416."],[141.0,"sulfide inclusions dissolve in acid, leaving small pits."],[148.0,"attacked and turn black. This is called flash attack."],[154.0,"these grades, and sometimes inhibitors are added to the acid."],[160.0,"The rule: when ordering passivation, state the exact stainless grade and the standard."],[167.0,"Electropolishing is the opposite of electroplating."],[171.0,"instead of adding metal, the current dissolves metal from the surface."],[177.0,"The bath is usually a mix of phosphoric acid and sulfuric acid."],[182.0,"The secret is in the peaks: a viscous layer of solution forms near the surface."],[186.0,"concentrates there, and they dissolve faster than the valleys."],[193.0,"microscopic level, and Ra drops, often by about half."],[199.0,"so electropolishing also improves corrosion resistance, and replaces passivation."],[208.0,"Electropolishing gives a surface that's easy to clean, with less room for bacteria and residue."],[214.0,"piping, medical devices, and vacuum and semiconductor systems."],[222.0,"on corners and edges, and the burrs dissolve first. But the corners also get rounded."],[231.0,"It gives a bright, uniform look, and a smooth surface where less builds up."],[236.0,"scratches, and it doesn't fix defects. It smooths what's there."],[243.0,"A rough surface with a deep scratch comes out bright, with a bright scratch."],[248.0,"So the surface is prepared first, by polishing or grinding, as required."],[253.0,"microns up to tens of microns, depending on time and current."],[261.0,"With tight dimensions, this has to be accounted for in advance."],[265.0,"And the removal isn't uniform: on corners and edges the current is stronger and removes more,"],[271.0,"and inside holes and internal corners the current is weak and removes less."],[277.0,"tubing, a special cathode is made that goes inside."],[283.0,"current, and a mark will remain there. The drawing should state where that's allowed."],[290.0,"pickling or grinding before passivation. Electropolishing removes it only if it's thin."],[300.0,"surface during passivation, and pitting in free- machining stainless."],[308.0,"Rust spots, when the part wasn't cleaned well enough before passivation."],[312.0,"the wrong place, and corners more rounded than allowed."],[323.0,"And what drives the cost? Passivation is usually inexpensive, and it's done in batches."],[329.0,"Testing and certificates per the standard add a fixed cost to every order."],[334.0,"racking and an electrical connection, and internal surfaces need a special cathode."],[342.0,"A specific Ra requirement also means mechanical preparation before the process."],[347.0,"And removing weld heat tint is priced as an extra step."],[351.0,"Next chapter, conversion coatings: phosphating and black oxide."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch04_Phosphating_and_Black_Oxide"]={"t":"Phosphating and Black Oxide","l":[[5.0,"In chapter 0 we saw that a conversion coating doesn't apply a layer of foreign material."],[10.0,"The surface layer of the part itself turns into a different compound."],[14.0,"Today we'll meet two conversion coatings for steel: phosphating and black oxide."],[19.0,"Both are thin and inexpensive, and both need a partner: oil, wax, or paint."],[26.0,"On their own, they give very little corrosion protection."],[29.0,"The bath is a solution of phosphoric acid, with salts of metals like zinc, manganese, or iron."],[36.0,"The acid lightly attacks the steel, and near the surface the acidity drops."],[41.0,"Then insoluble phosphate salts precipitate, and grow on the surface as dense crystals."],[47.0,"The result is a gray, matte, porous layer that's firmly anchored to the metal."],[53.0,"And the porosity is the advantage: paint grips it like an anchor, and oil soaks into it and stays."],[59.0,"activation step that makes the crystals small and dense,"],[67.0,"the phosphating bath, rinsing, and finally oil or paint."],[73.0,"used mainly as a paint base, on paint lines for sheet metal parts."],[80.0,"Zinc phosphate is the most common."],[83.0,"As a thin layer, it's an excellent paint base, for example in the automotive industry."],[88.0,"And as a heavier layer, with oil, it gives corrosion protection to fasteners and machined parts."],[93.0,"Manganese phosphate gives the thickest and darkest layer, gray to black."],[98.0,"It's used for wear: gears, pistons, camshafts, and parts that rub during break-in."],[104.0,"The layer holds oil and prevents galling."],[107.0,"in grams per square meter, not by thickness. The thickness is usually a few microns."],[115.0,"manganese phosphate, and TT-C-490 for paint pretreatment."],[125.0,"alkaline, oxidizing salts, at around 140 degrees Celsius."],[133.0,"The iron at the surface turns into magnetite, a black iron oxide."],[138.0,"The layer is very thin, about one micron, so it hardly changes dimensions."],[143.0,"That's why precision parts are blackened: tools, gauges, fasteners, and molds."],[148.0,"The look is black, matte or glossy, depending on the part's finish before blackening."],[154.0,"And most important: black oxide without oil gives almost no protection."],[159.0,"After blackening, the part is dipped in oil or wax, and only the combination protects."],[164.0,"Hot black oxide, at around 140 degrees, is true black oxide: a conversion layer."],[171.0,"Mid-temperature black oxide, a little above 100 degrees, is safer to work with."],[177.0,"conversion, but a chemical layer of copper and selenium deposited on the surface."],[185.0,"It's weaker, and used mainly for touch-up and appearance."],[189.0,"There are black oxide methods for steel, cast iron, and stainless steel."],[194.0,"Aluminum has no black oxide. It's made black by dyed anodizing, which we'll meet in chapter 6."],[201.0,"And the common standard is MIL-DTL-13924, with classes for steel and for stainless steel."],[208.0,"Black oxide: about one micron, for precision parts and appearance, always with oil."],[215.0,"Iron phosphate: the thinnest of the phosphates, as a paint base."],[220.0,"Zinc phosphate: a few microns, as a paint base, or for corrosion with oil."],[225.0,"And manganese phosphate: the thickest and darkest, for wear and break-in."],[231.0,"The rule: a precision part that only needs to be black and lightly protected gets black oxide."],[237.0,"phosphate, and a part that rubs gets manganese phosphate."],[245.0,"The black oxide bath is a hot alkaline salt."],[248.0,"joints later seeps out as a white powder, and starts corrosion."],[255.0,"and zinc, so each part is blackened separately, before assembly."],[263.0,"steel there's also a risk of hydrogen embrittlement."],[269.0,"As we Saw in chapter 1, a bake is done after the process."],[273.0,"type of oil or wax, the standard and type, and for phosphate, the required coating weight."],[282.0,"black oxide, when the bath is too hot or the concentration is wrong."],[291.0,"Uneven color from poor cleaning, and white powder from trapped salts."],[295.0,"Rust, when there isn't enough oil or it's washed off."],[298.0,"And in phosphate: coarse, uneven crystals, when the activation step is skipped."],[304.0,"usually inexpensive, and are done in baskets and barrels, many parts at once."],[312.0,"Large or delicate parts need individual racking, and that adds cost."],[317.0,"like salt spray and coating weight, and certificates."],[324.0,"not be coated. That's less common, because the layers are thin."],[331.0,"chromate, Alodine, and alternatives without hexavalent chromium."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch05_Aluminum_Conversion_Coatings"]={"t":"Aluminum Conversion Coatings","l":[[5.0,"oxide layer, a few nanometers thick, which protects it to some degree."],[12.0,"suffer from corrosion much more than pure aluminum."],[21.0,"The natural layer is also uneven, and paint doesn't bond to it well."],[25.0,"thin, cheap, fast layer that gives corrosion protection and an excellent paint base."],[34.0,"And unlike anodizing, it keeps the surface electrically conductive."],[39.0,"in or sprayed with an acidic solution of chromium compounds."],[46.0,"and forms a gel-like layer of chromium and aluminum oxides."],[53.0,"about one micron, and it hardly changes dimensions."],[60.0,"Its color is gold, greenish, or iridescent, and in other versions almost clear."],[66.0,"The process is short: a few minutes, at room temperature."],[70.0,"and Bonderite, and in industry it's also called chem film."],[78.0,"In traditional chromate, the layer contains water- soluble hexavalent chromium."],[83.0,"moisture, and restores protection to the exposed spot."],[91.0,"aerospace standard for years, and it's also why it's hard to replace."],[98.0,"The main standard is MIL-DTL-5541."],[102.0,"contains hexavalent chromium, and Type II has no hexavalent chromium."],[110.0,"And if no type is specified, the default in the standard is Type I."],[115.0,"The Class tells you the function."],[117.0,"Class 1A gives maximum corrosion protection, for a bare part or as a paint base."],[123.0,"surfaces, and for enclosures that need electromagnetic shielding."],[133.0,"Both classes must pass 168 hours of salt spray, and Class 3 is also tested for electrical resistance."],[141.0,"means: no hexavalent chromium, and electrically conductive."],[150.0,"toxic and carcinogenic, and its use is more and more restricted."],[157.0,"The main alternative is trivalent chromium, TCP, originally developed by the US Navy."],[163.0,"Today there are such processes approved for Type ll, in both classes."],[168.0,"chromate, but they don't have the same self- healing."],[174.0,"mainly as paint pretreatment, on industrial production lines."],[182.0,"almost clear, so it's hard to see by eye whether the part was coated at all."],[189.0,"Some solutions add a faint tint especially for this."],[193.0,"which removes the natural oxide layer and residue,"],[201.0,"rinsing, the chromate bath, rinsing, and drying."],[204.0,"easily, so for about the first day the part is handled carefully."],[211.0,"about 60 degrees Celsius, because high heat dries out the gel and reduces protection."],[221.0,"there are pens and brushes of solution for local touch-up."],[228.0,"and only the grounding and contact surfaces get chromate."],[239.0,"That requires masking, and the areas must be marked on the drawing."],[243.0,"machining after coating exposes bare aluminum, and needs local touch-up."],[251.0,"coating, and not to touch the surface with bare hands."],[257.0,"On high-copper alloys and castings the result is less uniform, and better preparation is needed."],[262.0,"and class, which surfaces must be conductive, and which areas are masked."],[270.0,"off, when the layer is too thick or rinsing is insufficient."],[277.0,"Uneven color or stains, from poor deoxidizing, or from differences between alloys."],[283.0,"scratches from handling while the layer is still soft."],[288.0,"And electrical resistance too high in Class 3."],[292.0,"And what drives the cost? The process itself is cheap and fast, and it's done in batches on racks."],[299.0,"Masking for selective coating, like anodizing with chromate areas, adds cost."],[305.0,"resistance, the certificates, and manual local touch-ups."],[312.0,"Next chapter: regular and hard anodizing, dyeing, and sealing."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch06_Anodizing"]={"t":"Anodizing","l":[[5.0,"Anodizing is an electrochemical conversion coating."],[8.0,"The part is connected to the positive pole, the anode, and that's where the name comes from."],[13.0,"surface into aluminum oxide: a hard ceramic layer that's part of the metal itself."],[22.0,"The layer grows out of the part: some of it inward, and some outward."],[30.0,"The result protects against corrosion, resists wear better, insulates electrically, and can be dyed."],[37.0,"cells, and each cell has a pore, perpendicular to the surface."],[44.0,"there's a thin, dense layer with no pores, called the barrier layer."],[51.0,"dye goes into them, but if they aren't sealed, moisture and dirt get in too."],[60.0,"The main standard is MIL-PRF-8625."],[64.0,"Type | is chromic acid anodizing: a very thin layer, up to a few microns."],[70.0,"aerospace for sensitive parts. There are also versions without chromic acid."],[78.0,"Type Il is regular anodizing, in sulfuric acid, and the most common: usually 5 to 25 microns."],[86.0,"It suits protection and appearance, and it's easy to dye."],[90.0,"Type Ill is hard anodizing, or hard anodize."],[93.0,"close to zero degrees, and at a higher current density."],[102.0,"The layer is dense and thick, and the default in the standard is about 50 microns."],[108.0,"And its hardness is comparable to hardened tool steel."],[111.0,"And the Class tells you whether the layer is dyed: Class 1 is undyed, and Class 2 is dyed."],[118.0,"Only the part that grows outward changes the dimension."],[121.0,"outward, and two thirds inward. In Type Ill, it's about half and half."],[129.0,"For example, 50 microns of hard anodizing adds about 25 microns per side."],[135.0,"An outside diameter grows by about 50 microns, and a bore shrinks by the same amount."],[140.0,"diameter changes by about 4 times the growth per side."],[147.0,"In our example that's about 100 microns, and that's enough to stop a bolt from going in."],[153.0,"reduced in advance in machining, and threads are usually masked."],[164.0,"Right after anodizing, while the pores are open, the part is dipped in dye."],[169.0,"The dye enters the pores and stays inside the layer."],[173.0,"Organic dyes give a wide range of colors, but some of them fade in sunlight."],[177.0,"salts is usually used: more durable, but in limited colors, like bronze and black."],[186.0,"In Type Ill the layer is naturally dark, so it's dyed only in dark colors."],[191.0,"In the last step the pores are closed, by sealing."],[194.0,"nickel acetate, and for regular anodizing also dichromate, which gives a yellow-green tint."],[202.0,"Sealing locks in the dye, and greatly improves corrosion protection."],[207.0,"unsealed, because sealing slightly reduces the layer's abrasion resistance."],[214.0,"grounding and contact surfaces, chromate areas are left, as we saw in the previous chapter."],[224.0,"when bent, so anodizing is done after bending, not before."],[231.0,"reduces fatigue strength, and in parts that see cyclic loads this must be taken into account."],[240.0,"The 6000 series, like 6061, takes anodizing very well."],[245.0,"castings, get a gray, less uniform, and sometimes thinner layer."],[253.0,"And in hard anodizing they're also more prone to burning, meaning local damage from high current."],[259.0,"a sharp corner a gap forms in it, and it cracks and flakes."],[266.0,"layer, and the standard has a table linking thickness to recommended radius."],[275.0,"Inside a narrow, deep hole the layer is thinner, because less current and solution reach it."],[280.0,"there will be no coating at the contact point. The drawing states where that's allowed."],[288.0,"into the bath: they burn, and contaminate the bath. They're installed after anodizing."],[298.0,"unsealed, masked areas, and whether dimensions apply after coating."],[309.0,"dark, damaged spot from locally excessive current."],[317.0,"Uneven color between alloys, between batches, or from uneven preparation."],[321.0,"Dye that fades in sunlight or from poor sealing, and powder on the surface from incorrect sealing."],[327.0,"because the growth wasn't calculated, and cracks at sharp corners and in parts bent after coating."],[335.0,"more than regular: the bath is cold, the time is longer, and cooling takes energy."],[344.0,"Masking of threads, grounding surfaces, and precision holes."],[348.0,"contact point, and complex parts need a special rack."],[355.0,"Color matching between batches, and between parts in the same assembly."],[359.0,"And tests and certificates: thickness, salt spray, and for hard anodizing, abrasion too."],[366.0,"thickness, throwing power, and hydrogen embrittlement."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch07_Electroplating"]={"t":"Electroplating","l":[[5.0,"negative pole, so it's the cathode. That's the opposite of anodizing and electropolishing."],[13.0,"The bath is a solution with ions of the plating metal, for example nickel, zinc, or copper."],[19.0,"gain electrons, become metal, and build a layer, atom by atom."],[26.0,"metal: it dissolves, and replaces the ions that were used up."],[33.0,"In other processes the anode doesn't dissolve, and the metal is added to the bath as a salt."],[39.0,"here we really do deposit a layer of a different metal on the part."],[46.0,"current and time. That's Faraday's law: more amps, more minutes, more metal."],[54.0,"area, So it's important to know the surface area of the parts in the load."],[61.0,"But not all the current goes into metal: some of it splits water, and creates hydrogen on the part."],[67.0,"and in hard chrome only a small part of it. That's one reason hard chrome is slow and expensive."],[79.0,"And that same hydrogen released on the part will come back to us later in this chapter."],[84.0,"prefers the shortest path: outside corners, edges, and protrusions."],[92.0,"The plating is thicker there, and inside slots, holes, and inside corners, it's thinner."],[98.0,"On a long part it looks like a dog bone: thick at the ends, and thin in the middle."],[104.0,"Each bath has a different throwing power: the ability to reach recessed areas."],[109.0,"Alkaline baths throw relatively well, and hard chrome throws very poorly."],[114.0,"into slots and holes, and shields that hide edges from the current."],[122.0,"Current thieves: pieces of metal near the edges, which draw the excess to themselves."],[127.0,"And the right orientation and distance of the part on the rack."],[131.0,"significant surfaces, the ones that matter for function or appearance."],[139.0,"On other surfaces the plating can be thinner, and without this definition, the part can't be inspected."],[145.0,"Orders of magnitude: decorative chrome is tenths of a micron, over a thicker nickel layer."],[151.0,"Zinc is usually a few microns up to tens, and hard chrome, tens to hundreds of microns."],[157.0,"It's measured by XRF, with X-rays, which suits most coatings."],[162.0,"on steel, and eddy current suits coatings on a non- magnetic metal."],[169.0,"And a microscopic cross-section is a destructive test, but the most accurate."],[174.0,"before the bath the part is cleaned, rinsed, and activated in acid, which removes a thin oxide."],[183.0,"On materials that are hard to plate, a thin, fast layer is applied first, called a strike."],[189.0,"Stainless steel gets a nickel strike, because its passive layer, from chapter 3, prevents adhesion."],[197.0,"Aluminum first gets a zincate treatment, a thin zinc layer, and only then is it plated."],[203.0,"And zinc die castings get a copper strike."],[206.0,"and peels, sometimes only after heating or bending."],[213.0,"strength steel causes embrittlement, and the fracture appears late, under load."],[221.0,"In electroplating the risk is greater: hydrogen forms on the part throughout plating."],[226.0,"And coatings like zinc and cadmium seal it in, making it hard for it to get out."],[231.0,"A bake is usually required for steels above about 1,000 megapascals, around 31 Rockwell C."],[238.0,"at least several hours, and for harder steels even a full day."],[246.0,"And the bake must start within about four hours of the end of plating."],[250.0,"machining, a stress relief bake is sometimes also done, before plating."],[257.0,"are dedicated tests for hydrogen embrittlement. When in doubt, the product standard governs."],[266.0,"and sharp inside corners get almost none. Radii balance both."],[274.0,"Blind holes and narrow slots get little plating, and trap gas and solution."],[279.0,"Add a vent hole, or define them as non-significant surfaces."],[283.0,"times the plating thickness, so specify a thread with plating allowance, or mask it."],[292.0,"surfaces, masked areas, and allowed contact points."],[302.0,"And also the bake requirement, and whether dimensions apply after plating."],[307.0,"Common problems: blisters and peeling, from poor adhesion."],[311.0,"Burnt plating, dark and rough at the edges, from too high a current density."],[316.0,"contamination, and nodules from particles in the bath."],[322.0,"Thin plating in recessed areas, and delayed fracture from hydrogen embrittlement."],[326.0,"is more time in the bath, and in hard chrome the cost is especially high."],[335.0,"Racking and fixturing, with special racks, auxiliary anodes, and shields."],[340.0,"Masking of threads, holes, and surfaces that must not be plated."],[344.0,"Baking, with oven hours and timing tied closely to the end of plating."],[348.0,"Tests and certificates: thickness, adhesion, salt spray, and hydrogen embrittlement."],[355.0,"And for silver and gold, the price of the metal itself."],[358.0,"Next chapter: zinc, zinc-nickel, and cadmium, for corrosion protection, including in aerospace."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch08_Zinc_Zinc-Nickel_and_Cadmium"]={"t":"Zinc, Zinc-Nickel and Cadmium","l":[[5.0,"sacrificially: it's more active, so it corrodes instead of the steel, even when there's a scratch."],[14.0,"coating in the world for bolts, nuts, and small steel parts. It's cheap, and it protects well."],[22.0,"The price is that the coating is consumed, and the thicker the layer, the longer it lasts."],[27.0,"When the zinc starts to corrode, a white powder appears on it: white rust."],[32.0,"It's not yet rust of the steel, but it's a sign the coating is working, and being consumed."],[38.0,"The common standard for zinc on steel is ASTM B633."],[43.0,"The Service Condition sets the minimum thickness."],[46.0,"SC1, for mild, indoor environments: 5 microns. SC2, for moderate environments: 8 microns."],[56.0,"SC3, for severe environments: 12 microns. And SC4, for very severe environments: 25 microns."],[66.0,"and colored passivates based on trivalent chromium."],[75.0,"Today, because of regulation, most work is done with trivalent passivates."],[80.0,"are not electroplated with zinc at all, because of the risk of hydrogen embrittlement."],[94.0,"added on top: a thin conversion coating, as we saw in chapters 4 and 5."],[103.0,"It slows down white rust."],[105.0,"added, which further improves protection, and sometimes friction too."],[112.0,"white rust appears, meaning until the zinc starts to corrode,"],[120.0,"itself is exposed. The specification should define both."],[127.0,"Zinc-nickel is an alloy of zinc with nickel, usually 12 to 16 percent nickel."],[132.0,"The protection is still sacrificial, but the alloy corrodes much more slowly than regular zinc."],[138.0,"Corrosion protection lasts several times longer, and it holds up at higher temperatures too."],[143.0,"system, and in aerospace and defense, as the main replacement for cadmium."],[153.0,"The standards are ASTM B841 for general industrial use, and AMS 2417 for aerospace."],[161.0,"little hydrogen, Low Hydrogen Embrittlement, for high-strength parts."],[169.0,"aerospace fasteners and connectors for many years."],[174.0,"It gives good sacrificial protection, even as a thin layer and in a marine environment."],[179.0,"cadmium bolt in an aluminum part causes almost no galvanic corrosion."],[187.0,"galling, and a consistent relationship between tightening torque and clamp force."],[196.0,"And it's easy to solder to, and it conducts electricity."],[199.0,"Type II with chromate, and Type III with phosphate, as a paint base."],[209.0,"13 microns. Class 2, 8 microns. And Class 3, 5 microns."],[221.0,"is toxic and carcinogenic, and its use is very restricted."],[229.0,"And in aerospace it's gradually being replaced by zinc-nickel."],[234.0,"protection is moderate, and it's less good with heat."],[240.0,"heat, and has reasonable galvanic compatibility with aluminum, but it costs more."],[249.0,"aerospace, especially against aluminum and on fasteners, but it's very restricted by regulation."],[259.0,"The rule: in general industry, choose zinc with a trivalent passivate."],[264.0,"aluminum, choose zinc-nickel. And cadmium, only when the specification or the customer requires it."],[274.0,"diameter changes by about 4 times the coating thickness."],[280.0,"bolt plated after manufacture, check that the thread still fits."],[289.0,"bake after plating. Zinc and cadmium seal in the hydrogen, so the bake must not be delayed."],[298.0,"temperatures, and near engines and exhausts, zinc-nickel is better."],[305.0,"standard, thickness or service condition, the passivate or Type, and a sealer if needed."],[313.0,"And also the bake requirement, and the salt spray requirement, for white rust and for red rust."],[320.0,"Common problems: early white rust, from a weak or damaged passivate."],[325.0,"areas, and blisters and peeling from poor preparation."],[332.0,"embrittlement, and uneven passivate color between batches."],[337.0,"cheapest, and bolts are plated in a barrel, thousands at once. Zinc-nickel costs more."],[347.0,"Cadmium is especially expensive, and few plants are still licensed to work with it."],[352.0,"Baking, sealing, and long salt spray tests add cost."],[357.0,"So does racking large or delicate parts that can't go into a barrel."],[361.0,"Next chapter: nickel, hard chrome, silver, and gold."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch09_Nickel_Hard_Chrome_Silver_and_Gold"]={"t":"Nickel, Hard Chrome, Silver and Gold","l":[[5.0,"Unlike zinc, nickel, chrome, silver, and gold are more noble than steel."],[10.0,"They don't protect sacrificially, but as a barrier."],[14.0,"a corrosion site, and sometimes the corrosion there is even faster."],[25.0,"So with these coatings, the thickness, density, and integrity of the layer are critical."],[31.0,"corrosion: hardness, wear resistance, conductivity, solderability, and appearance."],[43.0,"Nickel is the workhorse of electroplating."],[46.0,"Watts nickel is the classic bath: a light gray layer, fairly hard, with good protection."],[52.0,"Nickel sulfamate gives a layer with very low internal stress."],[57.0,"It suits thick layers, rebuilding the size of worn parts, and aerospace parts."],[62.0,"decorative plating. Its brightness is what gives the thin chrome on top its familiar look."],[69.0,"barrier that stops metals from migrating into each other, for example under gold on copper."],[79.0,"And two notes: electroplated nickel is magnetic, and some people are allergic to it on skin contact."],[86.0,"The AMS 2460 standard divides chrome into two classes."],[90.0,"Class 1 is decorative chrome: a very thin layer, tenths of a micron, over nickel."],[96.0,"resistance, and the corrosion protection comes mainly from the nickel underneath."],[104.0,"thick layer, usually tens to hundreds of microns, directly on the steel."],[112.0,"It gives very high hardness, wear resistance, and low friction."],[116.0,"Hard chrome has a network of micro-cracks. They hold oil and improve lubrication,"],[121.0,"but moisture can reach the steel through them, so hard chrome's corrosion protection is limited."],[128.0,"gear, molds, and for rebuilding the size of worn parts."],[135.0,"at the edges. So it's plated oversize, and then ground to size and finish."],[143.0,"Hard chrome lowers the fatigue strength of the steel."],[146.0,"So on parts under cyclic loads, shot peening is done before plating, as we saw in chapter 2."],[152.0,"low current efficiency, and a lot of hydrogen is produced."],[159.0,"On high-strength steels, baking is mandatory, and within about four hours of the end of plating."],[165.0,"which is why alternatives are sought, like thermal spray, which we'll meet in chapter 16."],[174.0,"Silver is the best electrical conductor of all metals."],[178.0,"It's used on contacts and connectors for high currents, and in RF components and waveguides."],[183.0,"There the silver acts as a lubricant, and prevents seizing."],[193.0,"sulfur compounds, and the black layer spoils the appearance and some contacts."],[202.0,"Sometimes it's protected with a thin gold layer, or an anti-tarnish treatment."],[208.0,"And a nickel or copper strike is usually applied under the silver, so it bonds well to the part."],[214.0,"Gold doesn't oxidize at all, so it keeps a low, stable contact resistance for years."],[219.0,"signals, and on circuits and electronic components, including gold wire bonding."],[227.0,"nickel, resists wear, for connectors that are mated and unmated many times."],[235.0,"And soft, pure gold is used for soldering and wire bonding."],[238.0,"migrates into the gold over time, and degrades the contact."],[247.0,"45204 in defense. They define purity, hardness, and thickness."],[258.0,"micron to a few microns, because every micron of gold costs money."],[266.0,"intact, so sometimes several layers are applied, for example copper, nickel, and chrome."],[276.0,"Each layer closes the pores of the one before."],[279.0,"With hard chrome, plan a grinding allowance, and define the final dimension after grinding."],[285.0,"And on parts under cyclic loads, add shot peening before plating."],[289.0,"areas are plated, to save cost. The rest is masked, or plated by selective methods."],[299.0,"requirement, and whether dimensions apply after grinding."],[311.0,"Common problems: corrosion through pores in a barrier layer that's too thin."],[315.0,"poor preparation, and fatigue failure in a chromed part without shot peening."],[323.0,"Tarnished silver, and gold that loses contact, when there's no nickel layer under it."],[328.0,"time in the bath, grinding after plating, baking, and shot peening."],[340.0,"For silver and gold: the price of the metal itself, so thickness and area are critical."],[346.0,"Selective plating and masking, to save precious metal."],[350.0,"And testing: thickness, hardness, adhesion, porosity, and hydrogen embrittlement."],[358.0,"Next chapter: electroless nickel."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch10_Electroless_Nickel"]={"t":"Electroless Nickel","l":[[5.0,"current decides where the layer is thick and where it's thin."],[11.0,"reaction takes place at every point the solution touches."],[17.0,"everywhere: on outside corners, inside holes, in slots, and in threads. That's its big advantage."],[28.0,"bodies, hydraulics, assemblies with internal passages, and parts with tight tolerances."],[39.0,"The solution contains nickel ions and a reducing agent, usually hypophosphite."],[45.0,"electrons to the nickel, and the nickel deposits on the part as metal."],[52.0,"Some of the phosphorus enters the layer along with it."],[55.0,"part is in the solution. That's why the process is called autocatalytic."],[66.0,"not pure nickel, and the phosphorus content determines almost all of its properties."],[75.0,"microns per hour, so it's controlled by time in the bath."],[84.0,"harder layer as plated, magnetic and easy to solder, but less corrosion resistant."],[94.0,"common: a balanced combination of hardness and protection."],[102.0,"almost amorphous structure, with no grain boundaries."],[108.0,"It gives the best corrosion protection, and it's non- magnetic, as long as it isn't heated."],[113.0,"phosphorus. For wear, low or medium phosphorus, usually with heat treatment."],[124.0,"The common standard is ASTM B733, and in aerospace AMS 2404 is also used."],[131.0,"to 3 percent. Type Ill, 2 to 4. Type IV, 5 to 9. And Type V, 10 and above."],[144.0,"Type | has no phosphorus requirement."],[146.0,"SC1, 5 microns. SC2, 13. SC3, 25. And SC4, 75 microns."],[160.0,"The Class sets the heat treatment after plating: Class 1 none, and Class 2 a hardening treatment."],[167.0,"The other classes are bakes to improve adhesion and to remove hydrogen."],[171.0,"means: high phosphorus, at least 25 microns, and no heat treatment."],[183.0,"Note: the classes of AMS 2404 are not the same as those of B733."],[189.0,"And the words electroless nickel alone, with no standard, define nothing."],[194.0,"As plated, electroless nickel is fairly hard."],[197.0,"layer crystallizes, and hard nickel-phosphorus compounds form."],[205.0,"and in Class 2 of B733, the requirement is at least 850 HK."],[214.0,"The price: the hardened layer becomes more brittle, and its corrosion resistance drops."],[221.0,"And high phosphorus loses its non-magnetic property."],[224.0,"So don't harden at high temperature when the part mainly needs corrosion protection."],[229.0,"And here too hydrogen is produced, so high- strength steels are baked, as in chapter 7."],[235.0,"On steel, electroless nickel bonds well after normal preparation."],[240.0,"electroplating, and for some alloys a bake after plating improves adhesion."],[249.0,"On stainless steel, an activation or strike is needed, because of the passive layer."],[255.0,"And small particles can be co-deposited in the layer, to create composite coatings."],[260.0,"carbides or diamond give extreme wear resistance."],[268.0,"outside diameter grows by twice the thickness, and every bore shrinks by twice the thickness."],[278.0,"times the thickness, and long internal bores get the same thickness, which is an advantage."],[286.0,"Areas that must not be coated need masking, and in internal bores that's hard."],[294.0,"And like any coating, the layer copies the surface underneath. It doesn't hide scratches and defects."],[301.0,"And the drawing should state the standard, Type, thickness or SC, and Class."],[306.0,"bake requirement, and whether dimensions apply after plating."],[314.0,"preparation, especially on aluminum and stainless steel."],[321.0,"Porosity and small pits, from contamination or particles in the bath."],[325.0,"Thickness out of spec, when the deposition rate changed and the time in the bath wasn't corrected."],[330.0,"Cracks in a hardened layer, under bending or load, and hydrogen embrittlement in hard steels."],[336.0,"electroless nickel bath is consumed, and needs close control and replenishment."],[346.0,"the bath, and masking, especially on internal surfaces."],[352.0,"Heat treatment and baking, and tests of thickness, phosphorus, hardness, adhesion, and porosity."],[360.0,"saves grinding and machining after plating, so electroless nickel often pays off, despite the price."],[370.0,"Next chapter: hot-dip galvanizing."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch11_Hot-Dip_Galvanizing"]={"t":"Hot-Dip Galvanizing","l":[[5.0,"In galvanizing, a whole steel part is dipped in a bath of molten zinc, at about 450 degrees Celsius."],[12.0,"The zinc in the bath is almost pure: more than 98 percent."],[16.0,"the layer is several times thicker, usually tens to more than a hundred microns."],[23.0,"And it's metallurgically bonded to the steel."],[26.0,"because the layer is so thick, it lasts for decades outdoors."],[33.0,"You see it on steel structures, guardrails, light and power poles, bridges, rebar, and large bolts."],[40.0,"The layer isn't uniform in composition: during dipping, zinc and iron diffuse into each other."],[46.0,"zinc-iron alloy, with decreasing iron content, and on top of them a layer of almost pure zinc."],[55.0,"scratches and knocks during transport and assembly."],[64.0,"The top layer is softer and more ductile, and it absorbs impacts."],[68.0,"adhesion is a metallurgical bond, not just a mechanical grip, as with paint."],[77.0,"preparation, as we saw in chapter 1: degreasing, rinsing, acid pickling, and rinsing."],[87.0,"and ammonium salts, which cleans off remaining oxide and protects the surface until dipping."],[96.0,"reaches bath temperature, and the alloy layers form."],[102.0,"drained off, and it's cooled in air or water. Sometimes a passivate is added."],[109.0,"electroplating. Thickness is set mainly by the steel itself: its thickness, and its chemical composition."],[120.0,"Silicon and phosphorus in the steel strongly affect the reaction with zinc."],[125.0,"layer grows very fast, and comes out thick, gray, dull, and sometimes brittle."],[134.0,"This is called the Sandelin effect."],[136.0,"0.04 percent, or between 0.15 and 0.25 percent, and phosphorus below 0.04 percent."],[149.0,"different between the parts. That's not a coating defect, but a result of the composition."],[159.0,"Thickness sets the protection life, not the appearance."],[167.0,"bolts, hardware, and small parts that are centrifuged."],[180.0,"And in Europe and worldwide, ISO 1461."],[184.0,"with no maximum. And the required thickness rises with steel thickness."],[191.0,"millimeters the minimum average is 85 microns, and for steel 3 to 6 millimeters thick, 70 microns."],[204.0,"proportional to layer thickness: a double layer lasts about twice as long."],[211.0,"The most critical part of the design is venting and draining."],[215.0,"Molten zinc has to get into every cavity and out of it, and the air has to escape."],[219.0,"A closed cavity with trapped air or moisture is a real explosion hazard in a 450 degree bath."],[226.0,"structure must have vent and drain holes, in the locations and sizes recommended by ASTM A385."],[237.0,"1, and large overlaps need venting, or a seal weld all around."],[245.0,"Dipping at 450 degrees creates thermal stresses, and thin, long, or asymmetric parts can distort."],[253.0,"symmetric design, help, and ASTM A384 deals with this."],[262.0,"steels, are prone to embrittlement after galvanizing. ASTM A143 gives guidance."],[273.0,"The layer is too thick for regular threads."],[275.0,"Bolts are galvanized, then spun in a centrifuge to throw off the excess zinc."],[280.0,"galvanizing. The internal thread is left without zinc, and the galvanized bolt protects it."],[290.0,"won't take on it, so welds are cleaned mechanically before galvanizing."],[297.0,"extend the life of the system, or for appearance. That's a duplex system."],[306.0,"the paint will peel. We'll meet this in the paint chapters."],[313.0,"Common problems: bare spots, from poor preparation or from slag."],[317.0,"A thick, gray, brittle layer from reactive steel, and distortion of thin parts."],[323.0,"Drips and excess zinc at corners and in holes, from poor drainage."],[327.0,"And white rust on parts stacked wet, before they had time to dry."],[331.0,"priced by weight, and it's relatively cheap per ton, so it pays off very well for structures."],[340.0,"A part that doesn't fit the bath in one dip needs a double dip, and that adds cost."],[345.0,"So does extra work: vent holes, cleaning welds, and straightening after distortion."],[352.0,"for nuts, and duplex, which adds a whole paint step."],[359.0,"Next chapter, we move into the world of paint, starting with the principles."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch12_Paint_Principles"]={"t":"Paint Principles","l":[[5.0,"Paint is an organic layer that protects mainly as a barrier, as we saw in chapter 0."],[10.0,"It has four main components."],[12.0,"everything together. It sets most of the properties: chemical resistance, UV resistance, and flexibility."],[24.0,"some also protect against corrosion, like zinc ina primer."],[31.0,"and spray, and then evaporates. And the additives improve flow, drying, and stability."],[42.0,"shrinks: the wet film is thicker than the dry film that remains at the end."],[50.0,"film remains. Fast and simple, but a suitable solvent can dissolve the paint again."],[59.0,"reacts with oxygen in the air, and the process is slow."],[67.0,"polyurethane, a base and a hardener are mixed, and they react to form a network."],[76.0,"This is the most common group in heavy-duty industrial coatings."],[80.0,"and we'll meet them in the chapters on industrial paint and powder coating."],[87.0,"With two-component paint, there's a limited working time after mixing, the pot life."],[92.0,"And between coats there's a recoat window. If you miss it, the next coat doesn't bond well."],[99.0,"In industrial coating, a single coat is almost never applied. A system is built, and each coat has a job."],[107.0,"for example a zinc-rich primer, which protects sacrificially, like galvanizing."],[119.0,"barrier, and lengthens the path of moisture and ions to the steel."],[126.0,"And the topcoat protects against sunlight and the environment, and gives the color and gloss."],[131.0,"Why not one thick coat? Thinner coats cure better, and have fewer defects."],[136.0,"And a pore in one coat almost never lines up exactly with a pore in the coat below it."],[142.0,"A familiar example: epoxy primer, epoxy intermediate, and polyurethane topcoat."],[148.0,"breaks it down and it chalks. Polyurethane resists sunlight, so it goes on top."],[157.0,"what remains after the solvent evaporates, and it determines the protection."],[165.0,"Dry film thickness equals wet film thickness, times the volume solids percentage."],[171.0,"solids, to get 100 microns dry, you apply 200 microns wet."],[179.0,"with a simple comb gauge, so the painter knows right away if it's in range."],[186.0,"gauge: magnetic on steel, or eddy current on aluminum, at many points, per a standard."],[196.0,"too thick can crack, sag, or not cure fully. So the specification has a minimum, and a maximum too."],[206.0,"The main standard for protective paint on steel is ISO 12944."],[212.0,"C1, a heated, clean building interior, and C2, rural, with low pollution."],[221.0,"C3, urban, or coastal with low salt, and C4, industrial, or coastal."],[228.0,"with high salt, and CX, offshore and extreme conditions."],[236.0,"major maintenance: low, up to 7 years, and medium, 7 to 15 years."],[247.0,"High, 15 to 25 years, and very high, more than 25 years."],[251.0,"And that's not a warranty period, but an estimate for maintenance planning."],[256.0,"harsher the environment and the longer the durability, the thicker the system."],[268.0,"chapters 1 and 2 we saw the cleanliness grades and the surface profile."],[276.0,"Paint on an unprepared surface will fail, no matter how good it is."],[280.0,"If the steel is colder than the dew point, a thin film of moisture forms on it, sometimes invisible."],[287.0,"degrees above the dew point, and within the paint maker's temperature and humidity limits."],[295.0,"and at the right time, and respect the drying times and the recoat window."],[303.0,"tape is applied and pulled off, and you check how much paint came off."],[312.0,"pulled with a measured force, to see at what force, and in which layer, it fails."],[320.0,"Thickness and appearance are also checked, and salt spray or humidity, as required."],[326.0,"each coat its type, color, and minimum and maximum dry thickness."],[336.0,"category and required durability, application conditions, and tests."],[345.0,"specification is sometimes enough, instead of writing it all out."],[352.0,"Common problems: peeling from poor surface preparation, or from a missed recoat window."],[358.0,"Blisters from moisture or salts under the paint, and sags and cracks from paint that's too thick."],[365.0,"corners and edges, and chalking and fading of epoxy in sunlight."],[373.0,"which in industrial painting is often the largest part of the cost."],[380.0,"The number of coats, because every coat is labor, material, and drying time."],[385.0,"pays off, because maintenance in the field costs much more."],[394.0,"edges, which get an extra stripe coat by brush, because that's where paint is thin."],[402.0,"Next chapter: liquid paint. Conventional spray, airless, electrostatic painting, drying, and baking."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch13_Liquid_Paint"]={"t":"Liquid Paint","l":[[5.0,"In spray painting, not all the paint reaches the part."],[8.0,"Some of it disperses into the air as mist, and is caught in the spray booth filters."],[13.0,"The percentage that stays on the part is called transfer efficiency."],[17.0,"is created, and how much solvent is released into the air."],[23.0,"and test method, so we'll compare the methods mainly in relative order."],[31.0,"In conventional spray, compressed air in the gun's air cap breaks the paint into very fine droplets."],[37.0,"and transfer efficiency is the lowest of all the methods."],[43.0,"pressure is limited, by the common definition, to 10 psi, about 0.7 bar."],[53.0,"The cloud is softer, less paint bounces off, and transfer efficiency goes up."],[58.0,"In many places, environmental regulations require HVLP or a more efficient method."],[64.0,"Both methods are used for fine finishing, metallic colors, small parts, and touch-ups."],[71.0,"In airless, no air is used to atomize the paint."],[74.0,"usually hundreds of bar, through a tiny tip, and the paint breaks into droplets because of its speed."],[83.0,"in one pass, and the method suits thick, high- solids paints."],[91.0,"It's the main method for painting structures, ships, and tanks."],[95.0,"The drawback: the finish is less fine, and thin coats are harder to control."],[101.0,"a little air at the cap shapes the fan, and the finish is finer."],[108.0,"cause serious injury. Never point it at the body, and never clean the tip by hand."],[119.0,"In an electrostatic gun, the droplets get an electric charge, and the part is grounded."],[124.0,"around it and reach the back side. This is called wrap-around."],[131.0,"That raises transfer efficiency a lot."],[134.0,"rotary bell is used, which spins very fast and atomizes the paint by centrifugal force."],[144.0,"Together with an electric charge, it's the most efficient method."],[148.0,"grounded, and the rack must be clean of old paint, or the attraction weakens."],[156.0,"electric field doesn't get in. This is called the Faraday cage effect."],[163.0,"inside, and there you need adjustment, or manual touch-up spraying."],[170.0,"the solvent to evaporate, before the next coat or before the oven. This is called flash-off."],[179.0,"Without the wait, solvent trapped under the surface bubbles out, and leaves small holes."],[184.0,"and mild heating, of a few tens of degrees, shortens their drying time."],[192.0,"usually between 100 and 200 degrees Celsius, per the paint specification."],[200.0,"withstand the temperature, and not every part fits in an oven."],[210.0,"itself, not just the oven temperature. A thick part heats slowly."],[217.0,"paint mist away from the operator and the part, and the filters capture it."],[225.0,"Clean air also matters for the finish, because dust sticks to wet paint."],[229.0,"and caps: threads, grounding surfaces, sealing surfaces, and precision holes."],[238.0,"Masking is manual work, and it's often a large part of the time."],[242.0,"is explosion-proof, and the operator wears a suitable respirator."],[252.0,"component polyurethane, respiratory protection is especially critical."],[261.0,"orange. The cause: poor atomization, or paint that's too thick."],[269.0,"Sags form from a coat that's too thick, or a gun held too close."],[274.0,"dry before they reach the part, because the gun is too far, or the solvent is too fast."],[284.0,"in the oven, when heating too fast or without a flash-off."],[291.0,"contamination on the surface, which repels the paint. This takes us back to chapter 1, to cleaning."],[301.0,"And dust and fibers come from an unclean booth, or clogged filters."],[307.0,"moderate efficiency, and suit small parts and quality finishes."],[319.0,"areas and structures. Electrostatic and rotary give very high efficiency, for production lines."],[329.0,"usually the largest manual task, and paint lost to the filters when transfer efficiency is low."],[339.0,"because every coat is another pass through the booth, and baking: oven, energy, and time."],[349.0,"and cosmetic requirements, which need a clean booth, a skilled operator, and inspection."],[358.0,"Next chapter: powder coating."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch14_Powder_Coating"]={"t":"Powder Coating","l":[[5.0,"Powder coating uses no solvent at all. The paint comes as a dry, fine powder."],[10.0,"Every particle already contains all the ingredients: resin, hardener, pigments, and additives."],[19.0,"goes into an oven. There the powder melts, flows, and cures into a continuous film."],[27.0,"powder that didn't stick can be collected and reused."],[39.0,"The powder is sprayed with an electrostatic gun, as we Saw in the previous chapter."],[44.0,"The particles are charged, the part is grounded, and the powder clings to it even before baking."],[49.0,"In a corona gun, a high-voltage electrode charges the powder. It's the most common type."],[56.0,"weaker, but the powder gets into inside corners better."],[65.0,"deep inside corners, because of the Faraday cage effect. It's a known limitation of powder coating."],[75.0,"is heated, and dipped in powder floating on a stream of air."],[82.0,"layer, hundreds of microns, mainly with thermoplastic powders."],[90.0,"With powder, whatever doesn't stick is drawn off, and collected in a cyclone and filters."],[99.0,"The collected powder is mixed back in with fresh powder, in small amounts."],[104.0,"So material utilization is very high, much higher than with liquid spray painting."],[109.0,"has to be cleaned, and sometimes a separate booth is kept for each color."],[117.0,"That's why powder coating is especially suited to runs in a single color."],[122.0,"layer, then cures by a chemical reaction, into a network that won't melt again."],[130.0,"ten to twenty minutes, and there are also low- temperature powders."],[141.0,"moment the part itself reaches temperature, not from when it enters the oven."],[148.0,"A thick part heats slowly, so it has to stay in the oven longer."],[152.0,"adhesion and flexibility. And over-cure changes the color, and causes yellowing."],[162.0,"The typical thickness is about 60 to 80 microns in one coat."],[166.0,"A very thin coat is hard to achieve with powder, so it's less suited to tight dimensions."],[171.0,"resistance, but sunlight breaks it down. It's used indoors, or as a primer."],[180.0,"for outdoor use, for gates, railings, and aluminum profiles."],[188.0,"Hybrid powder, epoxy-polyester, is intended for indoor use, and yellows less than pure epoxy."],[194.0,"And polyurethane gives a smooth, durable finish, for outdoor use."],[199.0,"powder primer is applied first, with a topcoat over it. Here too, as in chapter 12, it's a system."],[209.0,"Powder coating only works well on a well- prepared surface."],[213.0,"in chapters 4 and 5, and in other cases it's blasted before coating."],[224.0,"release gases in the oven. The gases bubble through the powder, and leave small holes."],[234.0,"coating, or special powders for outgassing materials."],[240.0,"grounding, ventilation, and safety standards for spray booths are a must."],[249.0,"point, so a hanging hole is designed somewhere out of sight."],[259.0,"masking must withstand the heat: special tape, and silicone plugs and caps."],[266.0,"surfaces need masking, or re-machining after coating."],[274.0,"And electrical grounding surfaces are masked, as with any paint."],[278.0,"work, so a radius is recommended, and openings as wide as possible."],[286.0,"color, thickness, pretreatment, masked areas, and tests."],[292.0,"coat or insufficient flow, and small holes from gases released by the material."],[300.0,"Poor coverage in inside corners, under-cure or over-cure, and peeling from poor pretreatment."],[306.0,"which is manual work, and color changes, which require cleaning the recovery system."],[315.0,"The oven, in energy and time, and thick parts need a longer time."],[319.0,"coated at all, and field touch-ups are hard, because powder needs an oven."],[326.0,"with almost no solvent and high material utilization, but it needs an oven, and fixed colors."],[338.0,"touch-ups, and gives thin coats, but it contains solvent, and has more waste."],[347.0,"Next chapter: electrocoat, or e-coat."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch15_Electrocoat_E-coat"]={"t":"Electrocoat (e-coat)","l":[[5.0,"water-based paint, and direct current is passed through it."],[12.0,"It works like electroplating, but with paint instead of metal."],[16.0,"particles are positively charged, are drawn to it, and settle on the surface."],[27.0,"The voltage is usually a few hundred volts, and it sets the film thickness."],[32.0,"layer, and it's also common on metal parts for agriculture, appliances, and metal furniture."],[41.0,"The secret is that the film being built is an electrical insulator."],[45.0,"drops, and the current moves on to areas not yet coated."],[52.0,"corners, and internal surfaces that spray can't reach."],[58.0,"electroplating in chapter 7. But here the film limits itself, so the thickness is very uniform."],[70.0,"doesn't get in the way like that, because the part is fully immersed in the solution."],[78.0,"and a conversion coating, usually zinc phosphate, as in chapter 4."],[86.0,"E-coat is very sensitive to the surface, and without good pretreatment, the protection suffers."],[92.0,"In the bath itself, the part stays for a few minutes, under voltage."],[97.0,"filtered from the bath itself, by a fine filtration called ultrafiltration."],[108.0,"paint is used: transfer efficiency is above 95 percent."],[115.0,"Celsius part temperature, for about 15 to 20 minutes."],[128.0,"much thinner than powder, but uniform and complete, even inside."],[136.0,"benchmark for corrosion protection, but like any epoxy, sunlight breaks it down."],[144.0,"layer, with a surfacer and topcoat over it. On internal surfaces, it stands alone."],[153.0,"light colors. It's used when the e-coat is the final layer, for example on black exterior parts."],[163.0,"cathodic is much more common, because it protects better against corrosion."],[175.0,"uniform thickness, and very high material utilization."],[182.0,"The paint is water-based, with little solvent, and the line is fully automatic, at a high rate."],[188.0,"not always enough for a harsh environment or for appearance."],[195.0,"and the whole bath is one color, so the color can't be changed easily."],[202.0,"coat suits high-volume production, not individual parts."],[214.0,"And here too, baking requires a part that can withstand the heat."],[219.0,"galvanizing in chapter 11, drain and vent holes are needed."],[225.0,"without paint, and paint and rinse water don't drain out."],[232.0,"rack, and there's no paint at that point, so it's placed somewhere unimportant."],[239.0,"Very tight joints and very narrow slots get little paint, or none at all."],[243.0,"the whole part is immersed. Usually plugs are used, or the area is machined after coating."],[253.0,"thickness, pretreatment, whether a topcoat goes over it, allowed contact points, and tests."],[264.0,"Common problems: unpainted areas in cavities, from an air pocket or missing drain holes."],[270.0,"A film that's too thin, from low voltage or an unbalanced bath."],[274.0,"bath or rinse, and small holes from gases, or from oil that wasn't cleaned off."],[283.0,"Weak adhesion and corrosion resistance, from poor pretreatment."],[287.0,"And runs of unrinsed paint, at holes and folds, from poor drainage."],[293.0,"line, which only pays off at high volumes, and the pretreatment, which is another long row of tanks."],[303.0,"keeping the paint quality up, the baking, and a topcoat, if needed."],[309.0,"coat is among the lowest there is, thanks to the high utilization and automation."],[319.0,"coat gives a thin, uniform film that reaches everywhere, in mass production."],[331.0,"Next chapter, we start on advanced coatings, with thermal spray."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch16_Thermal_Spray"]={"t":"Thermal Spray","l":[[5.0,"gas stream throws it at high speed onto the surface."],[17.0,"these splats, one on top of another, build the coating."],[28.0,"decomposing can be sprayed: metals, alloys, ceramics, and carbides in a metal binder."],[36.0,"several millimeters, so thermal spray is also used to restore the size of worn parts."],[45.0,"The big difference from welding: the material melts in the gun, not on the part."],[50.0,"temperature, usually up to about 150 degrees Celsius."],[57.0,"So there's no distortion, no change to the part's heat treatment, and no heat-affected zone."],[62.0,"But adhesion is mainly mechanical: the splats grip the roughness of the surface."],[67.0,"then blasting to create a profile, as we saw in chapter 2."],[74.0,"too, so it's cooled and the temperature is controlled."],[81.0,"It's simple and cheap, but the coating is more porous, and adhesion is weaker."],[90.0,"between them melts them, and compressed air throws the droplets."],[98.0,"method is very common for zinc and aluminum, for corrosion protection of structures and ships."],[107.0,"arc becomes a very hot plasma, which melts even ceramics with a very high melting point."],[116.0,"That's how ceramic coatings and thermal barriers are made, for example in turbines."],[121.0,"combustion accelerates the particles to supersonic speed."],[128.0,"coating is very dense, with porosity below one percent, and strong adhesion."],[138.0,"chrome: very hard layers, with very high wear resistance."],[147.0,"example on landing gear, hydraulic pistons, and shafts."],[160.0,"No hexavalent chromium bath, no hydrogen from the process, and usually better wear resistance."],[166.0,"grinding, usually with a diamond wheel, because the layer is so hard."],[173.0,"The coating is built from splats, not from crystals that grew out of the part."],[177.0,"much porosity there is, how many oxides, and how well the splats bonded to each other."],[187.0,"denser coating, and slow processes, like flame, give more porosity."],[198.0,"through them to the part, so the coating is sometimes sealed with a sealer."],[204.0,"And adhesion has a limit: under a strong point load, or impacts, the coating can spall."],[211.0,"cavities are hard to coat, and sometimes it's impossible."],[222.0,"surface, usually tens of centimeters, and whatever doesn't fit that distance doesn't get coated."],[230.0,"At sharp corners and edges the coating is weak and tends to spall, so a radius or chamfer is used."],[237.0,"And HVOF and plasma need a soundproof booth, dust extraction, and robotic gun motion."],[244.0,"is machined first, to the coating depth plus a grinding allowance."],[252.0,"At the ends of the recess a taper is made, not a square shoulder, so the coating won't spall."],[258.0,"shields, and whatever isn't coated must be protected from both blasting and spraying."],[265.0,"is defined, and the coater adds whatever is needed on top."],[271.0,"for example HVOF with WC-Co, final thickness and coated area, finish, and hardness."],[282.0,"and tests: adhesion, porosity, hardness, and thickness."],[290.0,"Common problems: spalling, from poor surface preparation or sharp corners."],[295.0,"Cracks from stresses in too thick a coating, or from accumulated heat."],[299.0,"unsuitable process, and corrosion under the coating, through connected pores, without sealing."],[308.0,"And material: carbide and ceramic powders are expensive."],[323.0,"process qualification and test pieces in every batch."],[332.0,"But restoring an expensive worn part often costs much less than a new one."],[337.0,"Next chapter: PVD and CVD, very thin coatings, built atom by atom."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch17_PVD_and_CVD"]={"t":"PVD and CVD","l":[[5.0,"or molecule by molecule, and the material reaches the part as a vapor."],[14.0,"microns: much thinner than any coating we've seen so far."],[20.0,"titanium nitride are several times harder than hardened steel."],[27.0,"The main uses are cutting tools, molds, punches, and wearing parts, and also decorative coating."],[34.0,"turned into vapor in a vacuum chamber, for example titanium or chromium."],[43.0,"This is done with an electric arc, or by ion bombardment, called sputtering."],[48.0,"fed into the chamber, for example nitrogen, and a ceramic layer forms, like titanium nitride."],[57.0,"about 200 to 500, depending on the coating, and there are also lower-temperature processes."],[66.0,"rotate on a fixture, so all sides get coated. Deep holes and narrow cavities get less."],[76.0,"long-established and very common, for tools and general use."],[84.0,"withstand higher temperatures, and suit high- speed and dry cutting."],[93.0,"friction, and common on plastic injection molds and forming tools."],[102.0,"TiCN is harder than TiN, for cutting and forming."],[107.0,"temperature, for wearing parts in engines and assemblies."],[118.0,"evaporated. Gases are fed into a reactor, react chemically on the hot part, and leave a layer on it."],[129.0,"The temperature is high: usually around 900 to 1,050 degrees Celsius."],[134.0,"sight problem, and adhesion is very strong, thanks to the temperature."],[141.0,"And the layer can be thicker, usually a few to more than ten microns."],[145.0,"re-hardening after coating, and there's a risk of distortion."],[155.0,"carbide, mostly on cutting inserts, which can withstand the temperature."],[162.0,"And there's also PACVD, a plasma version, at a lower temperature."],[168.0,"hardened tool steels, molds, and tools that are recoated."],[180.0,"reaches everywhere, with strong adhesion. Suited mainly to cemented carbide inserts."],[193.0,"tempering temperature gets PVD. Cemented carbide inserts for heavy cutting usually get CVD."],[203.0,"surface stays rough, so molds and wearing parts are polished before coating."],[212.0,"the chamber there's thorough cleaning, and inside the chamber there's also ion cleaning."],[220.0,"cutting tools the edge is slightly honed before coating."],[227.0,"support the coating. A very hard layer on a soft base cracks, like an eggshell."],[235.0,"dimensions don't need to change, and it's only accounted for on very tight fits."],[242.0,"And PVD-coated tools can be stripped, reground, and recoated."],[247.0,"example TiAIN or a specific type of DLC, and often the coater's trade name."],[256.0,"coat and to mask, for example no coating on threads or mating surfaces."],[264.0,"Preparation: polishing and finish before coating, and edge honing on tools."],[269.0,"The base material and its heat treatment, so the coater can confirm the temperature is suitable."],[274.0,"indentation test that looks at the cracks around the indent, and hardness."],[282.0,"insufficient cleaning or too sharp an edge, and coating cracking, from too soft a base."],[290.0,"Softening or distortion of the part, from a process temperature too high for the material."],[296.0,"uncoated areas, because of line of sight, or unsuitable rotation and fixturing."],[305.0,"hours of vacuum, heating, and cooling, and the price depends on how many parts fit in a batch."],[316.0,"Fixtures and masking for each part shape, preparation and polishing, and edge honing."],[322.0,"For CVD on steel, re-hardening after coating, and special and multilayer coatings cost more too."],[329.0,"But a coated tool often lasts several times longer, so the coating usually pays off very well."],[335.0,"Next chapter: special coatings, like PTFE, dry film lubricants, and more."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch18_Special_Coatings"]={"t":"Special Coatings","l":[[5.0,"corrosion, hard coatings, and coatings for appearance."],[11.0,"friction and galling where you can't lubricate, materials that stick to the surface,"],[20.0,"and corrosion protection for high-strength bolts, without the risk of hydrogen embrittlement."],[25.0,"known by the trade name Teflon, dry film lubricants, and zinc flake coatings."],[37.0,"Teflon: a polymer in which a carbon chain is completely wrapped in fluorine atoms."],[46.0,"Its friction is very low, almost nothing sticks to it, and it resists almost every chemical."],[52.0,"It works over a wide temperature range, up to about 260 degrees Celsius in continuous service."],[59.0,"The drawback: PTFE is soft, and wears easily under load and rubbing."],[65.0,"temperature, close to 400 degrees, to fuse into a film."],[71.0,"lower temperature, and resist wear better. They're known, for example, by the trade name Xylan."],[82.0,"and on fasteners that need to be assembled and disassembled without galling."],[93.0,"PTFE can be combined into a hard coating, giving both hardness and low friction."],[98.0,"are pores. They're filled with PTFE, giving a hard, durable surface with low friction."],[109.0,"And in electroless nickel, as we saw in chapter 10, PTFE particles can be dispersed in the layer."],[116.0,"The hard coating carries the load, and the PTFE provides the lubrication."],[121.0,"A dry film lubricant is a thin layer of solid lubricant, in a resin binder that holds it on the surface."],[128.0,"slide over each other, like a deck of cards, and it carries high loads."],[136.0,"Graphite withstands high temperatures, and PTFE gives low friction and prevents sticking."],[143.0,"The film is very thin, usually about 5 to 13 microns, so it hardly changes dimensions."],[149.0,"allowed: in vacuum, in space, in dusty places, and at extreme temperatures."],[159.0,"rubbing wear, fretting. In aerospace, the well- known standard is AS52772."],[171.0,"surface that provides grip, like light blasting or phosphating, as in chapter 4."],[179.0,"the data sheet. There are also air-drying types, for field repair."],[190.0,"On internal surfaces, like internal threads and bores, it's hard to spray a uniform film."],[196.0,"longer, and a hard base extends the life of the film."],[202.0,"and standard, thickness, areas to coat, preparation, and baking temperature and time."],[210.0,"and aluminum in a binder, and is applied without electric current."],[217.0,"The zinc protects sacrificially, as in chapter 8, and the flakes also form a barrier."],[224.0,"It's applied by dip-spin, dipping and spinning ina basket, or by spraying, and then baked in an oven."],[230.0,"source of hydrogen embrittlement, and it suits high-strength bolts."],[240.0,"and salt spray results are especially good, usually better than electroplated zinc of similar thickness."],[249.0,"The standard for fasteners is ISO 10683, and well- known trade names are Geomet and Delta-Protekt."],[257.0,"PTFE solves sticking, friction, and chemicals, but it's soft, and needs a high bake temperature."],[264.0,"without oil, but it wears over time, and it's hard to apply evenly inside."],[272.0,"And zinc flake solves corrosion on bolts without hydrogen, but its thickness affects the thread."],[278.0,"thread that's multiplied by about 4. So plan clearance, or define a maximum thickness."],[287.0,"the part, and any coating already on it, can withstand the temperature."],[295.0,"cleaning or preparation, and a film that wore quickly, from too high a load or a soft base."],[304.0,"and incorrect baking, which leaves a soft film or damages the part."],[311.0,"And missing coverage in internal threads and bores."],[314.0,"masking when only certain areas are coated, and baking."],[323.0,"testing of thickness, friction, adhesion, and salt spray."],[330.0,"would otherwise require changing the whole design."],[336.0,"sure the coating was really done the way we asked."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch19_Testing_and_Standards"]={"t":"Testing and Standards","l":[[5.0,"A good coating and a bad coating often look the same."],[8.0,"enough, whether the layer adheres, or whether hydrogen got into the steel."],[17.0,"So every requirement on the drawing needs a test method, and an acceptance criterion."],[22.0,"measured, at how many points, and what passes, turn it into a requirement you can verify."],[33.0,"adhere? How well does it protect? And did it harm the part?"],[42.0,"coating on steel, like zinc, paint, or chrome. It's fast, cheap, and common, per ASTM B499."],[53.0,"anodizing or paint on aluminum. For anodizing, the standard is ASTM B244."],[65.0,"together, like gold over nickel. The standard is ASTM B568."],[80.0,"thickness, and many measurement points, because thickness varies across the part."],[91.0,"polished, and the thickness is measured directly under a microscope."],[98.0,"and defects. It's the reference method, but the part is destroyed. The standard is ASTM B487."],[110.0,"thickness is calculated from the time, per ASTM B504."],[122.0,"often used: pieces coated together with the batch, which are the ones cut."],[132.0,"at the edge, burnishing, heat and quench, and tape."],[144.0,"In all of them, you check that the coating doesn't peel or blister."],[148.0,"much paint came off, per ISO 2409 or ASTM D3359."],[162.0,"and in which layer it fails, per ISO 4624 or ASTM D4541."],[176.0,"And for thermal spray, there's a pull test on a defined specimen, ASTM C633."],[184.0,"The most common test is neutral salt spray, per ASTM B117 or ISO 9227."],[192.0,"The panels sit in a salt fog, at a constant temperature, for a set number of hours."],[198.0,"what counts as failure: white rust, red rust, blisters, or peeling."],[208.0,"notes that salt spray results alone rarely correlate with service life in the field."],[216.0,"It's a test for comparison and quality control, not a prediction of how many years the coating will last."],[223.0,"humidity, and sometimes UV radiation too, are closer to field conditions."],[232.0,"coating itself is measured, Vickers or Knoop, at a low load, on a cross-section, per ASTM E384."],[248.0,"That's how hard chrome, heat-treated electroless nickel, and hard anodizing are checked."],[253.0,"For hydrogen embrittlement, ASTM F519 tests the process itself."],[259.0,"along with the parts, and are then held under a sustained load, usually for 200 hours."],[267.0,"that plate high-strength steels run this test regularly."],[273.0,"chemical tests that show where pores reach the base metal."],[281.0,"happens when one fails. ASTM B602 gives guidance."],[294.0,"requirements, and often attaches measurement results and a documented bake."],[303.0,"And on a new part, a first article is inspected in depth before a production run is approved."],[310.0,"and class, thickness and measurement method, and significant surfaces for inspection."],[318.0,"acceptance criteria, hydrogen testing and baking if relevant, and certificates."],[327.0,"method, like a good coating, or corrosion resistant. That can't be verified."],[336.0,"recess, and an uncalibrated gauge, or one meant for a different base material."],[347.0,"and destructive tests on an expensive part, instead of witness samples."],[355.0,"hydrogen test, and destructive tests that sacrifice parts."],[365.0,"plus first article inspection and process qualification."],[372.0,"But testing is much cheaper than a failure in the field."],[375.0,"selection. How to choose the right coating, and specify it correctly on the drawing."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch20_Design_and_Selection"]={"t":"Design and Selection","l":[[5.0,"The most common mistake is choosing a coating out of habit: we always put zinc here."],[9.0,"The right way starts with the question: what does the part need?"],[13.0,"outdoors, near the sea, or in contact with chemicals? Does it wear, rub, or gall?"],[22.0,"Does appearance matter? And what temperature does it work at?"],[28.0,"Each answer narrows the list, and only then do you choose a coating."],[33.0,"On ordinary steel, almost everything works: zinc, galvanizing, paint, and nickel."],[40.0,"hydrogen embrittlement, as we saw in chapters 7 and 8."],[46.0,"sometimes a hydrogen-free coating is better, like zinc flake, or thermal spray."],[55.0,"On stainless steel, passivation is usually enough, and if a coating is needed on top, it needs a strike."],[62.0,"anodizing, or paint, and electroplating directly on it is almost impossible without zincate."],[72.0,"material can soften in CVD, galvanizing, or powder coating."],[80.0,"Blasting, thermal spray, and PVD need line of sight, and don't reach deep holes."],[85.0,"everywhere, so they're better suited to complex shapes."],[92.0,"oven, and sometimes only liquid paint or thermal spray is left."],[98.0,"uniform coating, like electroless nickel, black oxide, or PVD, or plan an allowance and grinding."],[110.0,"suit an automated one, like e-coat, powder, or barrel plating."],[118.0,"restricted, so where possible, choose the alternatives."],[124.0,"moisture between them, the more active metal corrodes. We saw this in chapter 0."],[133.0,"aluminum part gets a suitable coating, like cadmium or zinc-nickel."],[141.0,"And the parts can be isolated with paint, a gasket, or a compound."],[146.0,"conductive coating, like chromate Class 3, and on the drawing they're marked separately."],[153.0,"And coat before assembly. Inserts and pins are installed after coating."],[159.0,"passivation per ASTM A967, and sometimes electropolishing."],[167.0,"per MIL-PRF-8625, and in the grounding areas, chromate per MIL-DTL-5541, Class 3."],[179.0,"A high-strength automotive bolt: zinc flake per ISO 10683, with no hydrogen."],[186.0,"A hydraulic piston rod: HVOF WC-Co, with grinding, as a replacement for hard chrome."],[193.0,"A cemented carbide insert: CVD."],[196.0,"ISO 1461, with paint per ISO 12944 on top, ina duplex system."],[207.0,"A good coating callout on the drawing answers all of the coating shop's questions."],[211.0,"per MIL-PRF-8625, and type and class, for example Type Ill, Class 1."],[222.0,"measured. And areas: what's coated, what's masked, and where contact points are allowed."],[232.0,"and whether dimensions are before or after coating."],[241.0,"And tests and certificates, as we saw in chapter 19."],[245.0,"understand what you wanted, the drawing isn't complete."],[252.0,"Send the drawing to the coating shop as early as possible, while it's still in design."],[257.0,"coating will come out thin, which drain holes are missing, and which masking will cost a lot."],[268.0,"corner, a drain hole, or moving the grounding surface."],[276.0,"On a new part, a first article is approved first, and only then a production run."],[282.0,"over time give a consistent result. Changing suppliers requires requalification."],[290.0,"coatings, metallic coatings, paint, advanced coatings, and testing and design."],[299.0,"material, the shape, the environment, and the coating are chosen together."],[311.0,"that didn't account for the process, or an unclear requirement on the drawing."],[323.0,"Thank you for watching the Coatings, Finishes and Paint course, from METAL HUB."]]};
